  "research_question": "Does one-step conditional identifiability survive the autoregressive rollout consumed by planning?",
  "conclusion": "No. One-step COJA makes history useful at the first predicted future, but the effect is not preserved by self-conditioned rollout. Reallocating part of the existing hidden native MSE to a true second autoregressive target repairs two-step planning and generalizes to an unseen third step without adding parameters or inference compute. A later single-factor test shows that rollout actions must also be relevant to the query/deployment support: unconditional replay actions retain part of the absolute rollout gain but erase the positive correct-history benefit and are substantially weaker than the deployment-matched zero hold. Finally, a single 4096-step run from the published initialization reproduces and strengthens the zero-hold result, ruling out a required two-stage schedule: relative to matched one-step COJA it improves h2 by 57.39 px and unseen h3 by 39.08 px, while standard PushT is +2.0pp with a paired 95% interval of [-2.67,+6.67]pp. The remaining tradeoff is the selected h1 subset, where RC is 3.01 px worse.",
